# Prepare the own reference before attempting the failing launch. A missing
# data selector is read at the reached reference-head, after p has been checked;
# a bare missing input would instead fail entry binding before data runs.
# Step 21.7 K2, S3: the same check over the real parent. Model is written inside D next to the reference place p that
# holds it and the input v; the launch is `D\Attempt()` and the record in its body is `p(v := data\other)`. The head
# p is checked first (it selects Model); the actual `v := data\other` names the input v of the real parent D (read
# before the launch: input_v 5), so its right-hand side is read -- and the field `other` of D does not exist: the
# read of the record fails, one reading reached admission and none is admitted, and the launch answers 4 (the rule
# of step 21.7 K2, as struct_actual_kind_refused; the former source answered 3). The reference p is read by an
# explicit write before the launch (prepared_p 1). A named actual that names no input of the real parent is refused
# before its value is read (place_reference_unknown).
# Norm: the book #structural-call (the data is parent(C)), #declaration-selection.
D:
    Model:
        int: v 1
    end: Model
    @: p Model
    int: v 5
    Attempt:
        p(v := data\other)
    end: Attempt
end: D
Observed:
    int: before 0
    int: prepared_p 0
    int: input_v 0
end: Observed
Main:
    Observed\before := 1
    Observed\prepared_p := D\p\v
    Observed\input_v := D\v
    D\Attempt()
